   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100051,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700357,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "The 'small data' cap is enforced, not advisory - and it is why this is never your application's database",
   "originalText": "The 'small data' cap is enforced, not advisory - and it is why this is never your application's database",
   "fontSize": 17,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "fn",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 1432,
   "width": 995,
   "height": 113,
   "strokeColor": "#64748b",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100052,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700364,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "Capping per-znode payload, and holding the whole tree to megabytes rather than gigabytes, is what keeps the entire dataset\nplausible to hold uncompressed in every replica's memory AND to write in full to every replica's write-ahead log on every\nmutation. A tree allowed to drift into gigabytes turns the already-expensive fsync-and-replicate-on-every-write path into a\nmulti-second operation - so THE EXACT MECHANISM THAT MAKES THIS SERVICE TRUSTWORTHY FOR COORDINATION DATA BECOMES THE\nMECHANISM THAT MAKES IT UNUSABLE the moment it is mistaken for a general document store. The strong consistency this design\npays for on every single write is a price worth paying at kilobytes-to-megabytes scale, and untenable at application-data\nscale.",
   "originalText": "Capping per-znode payload, and holding the whole tree to megabytes rather than gigabytes, is what keeps the entire dataset\nplausible to hold uncompressed in every replica's memory AND to write in full to every replica's write-ahead log on every\nmutation. A tree allowed to drift into gigabytes turns the already-expensive fsync-and-replicate-on-every-write path into a\nmulti-second operation - so THE EXACT MECHANISM THAT MAKES THIS SERVICE TRUSTWORTHY FOR COORDINATION DATA BECOMES THE\nMECHANISM THAT MAKES IT UNUSABLE the moment it is mistaken for a general document store. The strong consistency this design\npays for on every single write is a price worth paying at kilobytes-to-megabytes scale, and untenable at application-data\nscale.",
   "fontSize": 13,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "d7",
   "type": "line",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 1570,
   "width": 1680,
   "height": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#cbd5e1",
